Wolf4SDL: running on the Linux frame buffer console of a small board

Against https://github.com/ioan-chera/Wolf4SDL, branch wolf4sdl, commit
51b3694e58bad8c9be9cddba720f86a4749d469c (2011-10-08; a git copy of the
Wolf4SDL svn repository at the last revision that uses SDL 1.2).
Apply with: git apply Wolf4SDL-korvo.patch

Written for the ESP32-S31-Korvo-1 running esp32-s31-linux, which has no
sound device and whose screen is also the terminal the game is started
from.  The changes are offered under the GNU GPL version 2, the licence the
game is built under here (see license-gpl.txt; build with GPL=1).

id_sd.cpp
    With no sound device the game carries on without sound.  It used to end
    at start-up with "SD_PrepareSound(0): DigiList not initialized!".

id_vl.cpp, id_vh.cpp
    Double buffering is off unless --dblbuf asks for it, and the screen is
    asked for as a hardware surface: the game then draws straight into the
    frame buffer.  Where the screen cannot flip pages SDL answers a request
    for double buffering with a second copy of the screen (512 KB at
    640x400) that it copies across on every frame.
    The small pictures of the status bar are ordinary memory, not hardware
    surfaces: as those, SDL made them in the format of the screen.

id_vp.cpp, id_vp.h (new), id_vl.cpp, id_vh.cpp, wl_draw.cpp, wl_main.cpp
    The game draws at 320x200 unless --res says otherwise, and the picture
    is enlarged on its way to the screen: --scale whole (by the largest
    whole number that fits, the default), fill (the whole screen) or aspect
    (the largest 4:3 picture).  A copy of what is on the screen is kept, and
    only what differs from it is written, four source pixels at a time and
    two screen pixels to a store.  As released the game handed its whole
    picture to SDL on every frame, which converted every pixel of it, and
    filling a larger screen meant drawing the game larger.  On the board
    this was written for, where every store to memory is slow, a frame of
    the first demo went from 478,000 stores to 83,000.  --scale off leaves
    it to SDL as before; --screen gives the size of the screen where SDL
    cannot tell.  WOLF3D_CHECK_PRESENT in the environment makes the game
    read the screen back after every picture and stop if it is not what it
    drew: for tests.

id_ca.cpp, id_ca.h, id_pm.cpp, wl_menu.cpp
    A data file that is not there under its name in lower case is looked
    for in upper case, as copies from DOS and Windows have them.

wl_main.cpp, wl_game.cpp, wl_play.cpp, wl_draw.cpp, wl_def.h
    --timedemo <0-3> plays a demo as fast as it will go, prints the frame
    rate and ends.  --showfps turns on the frame counter that is already in
    the game.  A line with the frames drawn and the rate is printed at the
    end of every run.
    Before an error is printed the console is put back in text mode.

wl_fbcon.cpp, wl_fbcon.h (new), Makefile
    Messages are kept until the game ends: in graphics mode the console
    does not scroll, and what is printed lands on one line.
    A console guard: a second small process that waits for the game to end
    and puts the console back in text mode if the game did not (it was
    killed with SIGKILL, say, which is what the kernel does when memory
    runs out).  WOLF3D_NOGUARD=1 in the environment turns it off.
    --fix-console does the same by hand: from another login, or from a
    timer started before the game.
    Built for a RISC-V processor with Zba, Zbb or Zbs, the game tries one
    instruction of each at start-up and says what is missing, where it
    would otherwise stop with "Illegal instruction" at the first one it
    met, which could be in the middle of a level.

